description:
Path: crate-jmap-server/src/lib.rs:296-303. ClosureHandler is a pub struct with two pub fields (backend, call_fn) and no #[non_exhaustive] attribute. Once shipped on crates.io, any addition to this struct — a tracing context, a metrics handle, a clock source, a cancellation token, anything — requires a major-version bump because callers can construct the type with struct-literal syntax and pattern-match its fields:

  let h = ClosureHandler { backend, call_fn };
  let ClosureHandler { backend, call_fn } = h;

Compare BackendSetError<E> (backend.rs:325), which IS #[non_exhaustive] precisely so its variant set can grow without breaking consumers. Same standard should apply here.

Fix shape:
  #[non_exhaustive]
  pub struct ClosureHandler<B, C> {
      pub backend: Arc<B>,
      pub call_fn: Box<BackendCallFn<B, C>>,
  }
  impl<B, C> ClosureHandler<B, C> { pub fn new(backend: Arc<B>, call_fn: Box<BackendCallFn<B, C>>) -> Self {...} }

Now: callers using struct-literal syntax get a compile error today; the new() constructor is the supported path. Adding a future field updates the constructor signature only — that IS a breaking change but ONLY for the new() callers, and #[non_exhaustive] forbids the struct-literal path so the breakage is localized and obvious. Without #[non_exhaustive] the new field can be added as a builder method on ClosureHandler if the constructor stays Option<Field>-typed, or as a separate ClosureHandlerV2 type if not.

The current shape locks the type's surface to "backend + closure" for the entire 1.x lifetime. Reasonable extension targets that this prevents: per-handler tracing/metrics, per-handler timeouts, per-handler permissions cache.

Per workspace AGENTS.md "Canonical Templates" rule: jmap-server is the canonical foundation; every extension *-server crate re-exports ClosureHandler (verified at crate-jmap-mail-server/src/lib.rs:`pub use jmap_server::ClosureHandler;` and crate-jmap-chat-server/src/lib.rs same). Locking the shape locks all five extensions' shapes too.
